// Stationary iterative solver (issue #53): Jacobi for A x = b, K fixed sweeps.
//
// The Jacobi iteration in RESIDUAL form (so the diagonal need not be excluded from the
// sum) is  x^{k} = x^{k-1} + D^{-1} ( b - A x^{k-1} ),  i.e. per row
//   x^{k}_i = x^{k-1}_i + ( b_i - sum_j A(i,j) x^{k-1}_j ) / A(i,i).
// One sweep is a gemv (the reduction over j) plus an axpy-style update; the iteration
// index k is a third axis, and each sweep reads ONLY the previous sweep's x -- Jacobi is
// FULLY PARALLEL within a sweep. (Gauss-Seidel would instead read x^{k}_j for j < i, an
// intra-sweep triangular wavefront that serializes the sweep; see stationary.md.)
//
// Index space (i,j,k): i = row / unknown (0..N-1), j = column / reduction (0..N), k =
// iteration (0..K-1). j runs one plane PAST the reduction: j = 0..N-1 is the gemv reduction,
// and j = N is the UPDATE plane.
//
// BREAKING THE ZERO-SLACK FUSION. Naively the residual divide would fire at the reduction's
// finishing cell (i, N-1, k), reading the completed acc(i,N-1,k) at the SAME lattice point --
// a [0,0,0] self-dependence no linear tau can order (two computations, one point, one time).
// We move the update ONE PLANE UP, to j = N: xs(i,N,k) reads acc(i,N-1,k) via acc(i,j-1,k),
// now a translation [0,+1,0] with slack. The other multiply-accumulate reads take their
// operands one step back along each carry (am at k-1, held; bb/diag at j-1, propagated) --
// the gemv trick -- so every dependence has slack >= 1 and the system is LINEARLY SCHEDULABLE.
//
// SURE vs SARE. All arcs are translations EXCEPT one: the cross-sweep gather xs(j,N,k-1) in
// the reduction reads x^{k-1}_j across every row -- the honest matrix-vector broadcast, an
// AFFINE (i -> j) transpose. It cannot be pipelined into a uniform arc here because feeding a
// solved x back through a boundary would require an input face to read a recurrence variable,
// which the DSL forbids; so Jacobi is a SARE with exactly this one affine arc (down from two
// in the fused form -- the xs->xs self-broadcast is now a uniform k-carry). It is a benign
// FORWARD arc (a sweep reads only sweep k-1), so a linear schedule exists.
//
// SCHEDULE. Both legal: the FREE schedule is the parallel sweep (all N rows of a sweep finish
// together -- Jacobi's wide wavefront -- then the k-1 dependence serializes sweep after sweep);
// the LINEAR schedule tau = [-1, 1, 2N] wavefronts it as a plane (tau_i < 0 because the update
// plane's i = N halo is an influx face, so rows fire staggered rather than all at once).
//
// Run it:   dfactl --sure docs/SURE/stationary.sure                 (free schedule)
//           dfactl --sure docs/SURE/stationary.sure --schedule linear

N = 3; K = 8;

system ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, 0 <= j <= N, 0 <= k < K) {
    am(i,j,k)   = am(i,j,k-1);                                              // A(i,j), held across sweeps (fed k=-1)
    bb(i,j,k)   = bb(i,j-1,k);                                             // b(i), propagated along +j (fed j=-1)
    diag(i,j,k) = diag(i,j-1,k);                                          // A(i,i), propagated along +j
    acc(i,j,k)  = acc(i,j-1,k) + am(i,j,k-1) * xs(j,N,k-1);               // reduce sum_j A(i,j) x^{k-1}_j (affine gather)
    xs(i,j,k)   = xs(i,j,k-1) + (bb(i,j-1,k) - acc(i,j-1,k)) / diag(i,j-1,k); // residual update; correct at j = N
}

// tensor confluences
input  A[N][N+1] ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, 0 <= j < N+1, k = -1)  : am(i,j,k)   = A[i][j];   // A (+ a phantom 0 column j=N) held from k=-1
input            ((i,j,k) | i = N, 0 <= j <= N, -1 <= k < K)   : xs(i,j,k)   = 0;         // i=N halo: the reduction's j=N term is A*0, value unused
input  B[N]      ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = -1, 0 <= k < K)    : bb(i,j,k)   = B[i];      // b on the j = -1 face
input  Diag[N]   ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = -1, 0 <= k < K)    : diag(i,j,k) = Diag[i];   // A(i,i) on the j = -1 face
input            ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = -1, 0 <= k < K)    : acc(i,j,k)  = 0;         // reduction seed
input  X0[N]     ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, 0 <= j <= N, k = -1)   : xs(i,j,k)   = X0[i];     // initial guess x^{-1} = x^0
output X[N]      ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = N, K-1 <= k, k < K) : X[i] = xs(i,j,k);        // x after K sweeps (k=K-1)

tau = [-1, 1, 14];   // linear wavefront (tau_k >= 2N clears the affine gather; 14 >= 2*N for the N<=6 catalog/viz sizes)

// test bench:  strongly diagonally dominant A, exact solution x = [1,1,1].
//   A = [[10,1,1],[1,10,1],[1,1,10]],  b = [12,12,12],  Diag = [10,10,10],  x0 = 0.
//   K = 8 Jacobi sweeps converge to x = [1,1,1]. (A carries a phantom 4th column of 0.)
data A    = { 10,  1,  1, 0,
               1, 10,  1, 0,
               1,  1, 10, 0 };
data B    = { 12, 12, 12 };
data Diag = { 10, 10, 10 };
data X0   = {  0,  0,  0 };
